WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:04.460
Open Rack Wide, or O R W, expands the mechanical envelope for

2
00:00:04.480 --> 00:00:07.260
different equipment packaging and rack-scale integration needs.

3
00:00:07.700 --> 00:00:11.460
That creates opportunities, but also changes the questions asked of the site

4
00:00:11.580 --> 00:00:12.620
and the complete assembly.

5
00:00:13.140 --> 00:00:16.980
More width can affect internal arrangements, cable and hose routes, and service

6
00:00:17.060 --> 00:00:17.440
access.

7
00:00:17.900 --> 00:00:21.900
A larger or more integrated payload changes handling and structural considerations.

8
00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:26.460
None of those choices automatically establishes the electrical or thermal capacity of

9
00:00:26.480 --> 00:00:27.360
the installed system.

10
00:00:27.920 --> 00:00:31.680
Read the O R W base specification as a shared interface contract.

11
00:00:32.080 --> 00:00:36.880
Then identify the implementation that supplies external dimensions, payload and other design

12
00:00:36.920 --> 00:00:37.320
choices.

13
00:00:37.860 --> 00:00:42.320
This distinction is particularly important when a proposal mixes a base-specification name

14
00:00:42.360 --> 00:00:45.000
with a photograph and a rating from a different configuration.

15
00:00:45.400 --> 00:00:47.940
Keep the source and scope attached to each claim.

16
00:00:48.460 --> 00:00:52.240
The review should establish why the selected arrangement suits the deployment, not

17
00:00:52.280 --> 00:00:55.280
simply that wider equipment can be placed inside a wider frame.

